The proposed temp-file fix is correct. Concrete replacement for dut.sh lines 96-101 (line 102 unchanged), keeping the existing log-file naming and adding the kept-copy note to the failure messages:

    tmp="$OUT/.$file.tmp"; rm -f "$tmp"
    ( cd "$OUT" && apt-get download "tron=$want" >"$OUT/apt-download-$(date -u +%H%M%S).log" 2>&1 && mv -f "tron_${want}_amd64.deb" "$tmp" ) \
      || { c=$(ls /var/cache/apt/archives/tron_${want}_amd64.deb 2>/dev/null | head -1); [ -n "$c" ] && cp -f "$c" "$tmp"; }
    [ -s "$tmp" ] || { rm -f "$tmp"; say "could not save the deb $want as $file (download and apt cache both failed); existing $file kept, version $(dpkg-deb -f "$OUT/$file" Version 2>/dev/null || echo none)"; exit 1; }
    v=$(dpkg-deb -f "$tmp" Version 2>/dev/null)
    [ "$v" = "$want" ] || { rm -f "$tmp"; say "downloaded deb has version '$v', wanted $want; existing $file kept"; exit 1; }
    mv -f "$tmp" "$OUT/$file"

Behaviour: the existing saved copy is replaced only after the new file exists and its Version matches $want; the failure path leaves $OUT/$file untouched. No change needed in campaign.sh; the .orig llama driver (dut.sh:82) carries the same ordering if it is ever reused.
